1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,457
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,457 --> 00:00:04,772
Episode 13.

3
00:00:04,772 --> 00:00:07,457
The Invisible Ant Highway.

4
00:00:07,457 --> 00:00:10,298
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,298 --> 00:00:15,397
What can guide many ants along a trail even when there is no visible line?

6
00:00:15,397 --> 00:00:16,889
Ready?

7
00:00:16,889 --> 00:00:19,062
Let us find out.

8
00:00:19,062 --> 00:00:24,573
After a picnic, Maya notices ants walking along the garden path.

9
00:00:24,573 --> 00:00:27,386
Some go toward a tiny crumb.

10
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:30,823
Others return toward a crack near a tree.

11
00:00:30,823 --> 00:00:33,082
Who painted their road?

12
00:00:33,082 --> 00:00:35,113
Leo asks.

13
00:00:35,113 --> 00:00:37,883
I cannot see any lines.

14
00:00:37,883 --> 00:00:40,326
Pip studies the ground.

15
00:00:40,326 --> 00:00:42,613
No road signs.

16
00:00:42,613 --> 00:00:45,000
No traffic lights.

17
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,528
No parking spaces.

18
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:52,301
Ms. Park explains that many ants can leave chemical trails.

19
00:00:52,301 --> 00:00:56,860
Other ants detect those signals with their antennae and follow them.

20
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:59,758
So their road can be made of smell?

21
00:00:59,758 --> 00:01:01,875
Maya asks.

22
00:01:01,875 --> 00:01:06,178
That is a helpful way to imagine it, says Ms. Park.

23
00:01:06,178 --> 00:01:09,673
The children watch without blocking the path.

24
00:01:09,673 --> 00:01:14,403
An ant meets another ant, pauses, and continues.

25
00:01:14,403 --> 00:01:17,329
Several ants work near the crumb.

26
00:01:17,329 --> 00:01:20,809
Leo imagines a tiny radio station.

27
00:01:20,809 --> 00:01:23,494
Attention, everyone.

28
00:01:23,494 --> 00:01:26,590
Crumb traffic is heavy near the tree.

29
00:01:26,590 --> 00:01:30,795
Pip offers to build a bridge, but Maya stops him.

30
00:01:30,795 --> 00:01:33,210
Their path is working.

31
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:35,497
We can just observe.

32
00:01:35,497 --> 00:01:38,607
The club draws the route in a notebook.

33
00:01:38,607 --> 00:01:44,531
They mark where ants enter and leave, then count how many pass one spot in a minute.

34
00:01:44,531 --> 00:01:47,215
The next minute is different.

35
00:01:47,215 --> 00:01:51,505
Our first count was not the whole story, Maya says.

36
00:01:51,505 --> 00:01:56,903
Before leaving, they collect their picnic trash and keep the area clean.

37
00:01:56,903 --> 00:01:59,573
Pip looks back at the ants.

38
00:01:59,573 --> 00:02:05,767
Their highway has no signs, he says, but it seems to have plenty of information.

39
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:08,806
Now, a little more to discover.

40
00:02:08,806 --> 00:02:13,707
Many ant species use chemicals called pheromones to communicate.

41
00:02:13,707 --> 00:02:18,678
A trail can help other ants find a route, such as a path to food.

42
00:02:18,678 --> 00:02:22,457
Ants detect chemical signals with their antennae.

43
00:02:22,457 --> 00:02:29,772
Different species use different combinations of signals, so one simple rule does not describe every ant.

44
00:02:29,772 --> 00:02:32,741
Let us hear the main idea again.

45
00:02:32,741 --> 00:02:36,448
The ants followed a route that people could not see.

46
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:39,446
Chemical signals helped guide them.

47
00:02:39,446 --> 00:02:45,482
The children watched, drew a map, and compared counts without disturbing the trail.

48
00:02:45,482 --> 00:02:48,593
Here are three useful expressions.

49
00:02:48,593 --> 00:02:51,860
You can listen, or say them with me.

50
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:54,332
They are following each other.

51
00:02:54,332 --> 00:02:56,803
They are following each other.

52
00:02:56,803 --> 00:02:59,545
Let us watch without touching.

53
00:02:59,545 --> 00:03:02,286
Let us watch without touching.

54
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:04,715
What changed this time?

55
00:03:04,715 --> 00:03:07,144
What changed this time?

56
00:03:07,144 --> 00:03:09,857
Remember our listening question?

57
00:03:09,857 --> 00:03:14,957
What can guide many ants along a trail even when there is no visible line?

58
00:03:14,957 --> 00:03:17,187
Here is the answer.

59
00:03:17,187 --> 00:03:20,099
Chemical signals can guide them.

60
00:03:20,099 --> 00:03:23,835
Other ants detect those signals with their antennae.

61
00:03:23,835 --> 00:03:27,656
And here is a question just for your imagination.

62
00:03:27,656 --> 00:03:34,673
What invisible information do people use, such as sounds, smells, or wireless signals?

63
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:37,926
There is more than one possible answer.

64
00:03:37,926 --> 00:03:41,321
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
